QB["hw-power"] = (QB["hw-power"] || []).concat([
["Conduction modes", "What's the difference between continuous and discontinuous conduction mode?", "**Why they ask:** CCM versus DCM changes everything about a converter.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In continuous conduction mode the inductor current never falls to zero during a switching cycle — there's always current flowing. In discontinuous mode the current ramps down to zero and sits there for part of the cycle before the next switch-on. CCM gives lower peak and RMS currents, lower ripple and easier filtering, so it suits higher loads; DCM happens at light load or with a small inductor and changes the control-to-output behavior — the transfer function and the compensation differ. A converter often moves between them with load, so I design the loop to be stable in both.\"\n\nTip: CCM = current never zero (high load); DCM = current hits zero (light load) with a different transfer function.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why does the control loop change between CCM and DCM?","The small-signal transfer function differs — DCM loses a pole and behaves more like first-order — so compensation stable in CCM must also hold in DCM."]}],
["Synchronous rectification", "What is synchronous rectification and when is it worth it?", "**Why they ask:** efficiency fundamentals.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Instead of a diode on the low side, synchronous rectification uses a MOSFET turned on in place of the diode's conduction. A diode drops around 0.4 to 0.7 volts and burns that times the current as loss; a MOSFET's on-resistance can drop far less, so at high current and low output voltage the efficiency gain is large. The cost is a second switch, a gate driver, and the control complexity of dead time to avoid shoot-through. It's worth it for low-voltage, high-current rails where the diode loss would dominate, and less so at very light load where the MOSFET can let current flow backward unless you add diode-emulation.\"\n\nTip: Replace the diode's fixed drop with a low-Rds(on) FET — big win at low voltage, high current.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What is dead time and why does it matter?","A brief gap where both switches are off during a transition, to prevent both conducting at once (shoot-through); too much dead time adds diode loss, too little risks a destructive cross-conduction."]}],
["Gate drive", "How do you drive a high-side MOSFET in a buck converter?", "**Why they ask:** gate drive is where many designs fail.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The high-side N-channel FET's source swings up to the input voltage, so its gate has to be driven above the input to turn it fully on. The common approach is a bootstrap: a capacitor charged from a low-side supply through a diode when the switch node is low, which then floats up with the source to provide gate drive above the rail. I size the bootstrap cap for the gate charge, make sure the switch node goes low often enough to refresh it, and watch the gate-drive loop inductance because a slow or ringing gate drive causes loss and EMI. For always-on high side you need a charge pump instead.\"\n\nTip: Bootstrap cap supplies above-rail gate drive, refreshed each cycle the switch node goes low.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What happens if the switch node never goes low?","The bootstrap cap can't refresh and the high-side gate loses drive; a 100% duty requirement needs a charge pump or a different scheme."]}],
["Switching node", "The switch node rings and radiates. What causes it and how do you fix it?", "**Why they ask:** the hot loop is the #1 EMI source.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The ringing comes from the parasitic inductance of the high-di/dt loop — the input cap, high-side and low-side switches — resonating with the MOSFET output capacitance each time the node switches. Fast edges plus loop inductance equals a high-voltage ring and broadband EMI. The first fix is layout: make that hot loop as tiny as possible with the input cap right at the switches, so there's little inductance to ring. Then I can add a small snubber across the switch node, slow the gate turn-on slightly with a gate resistor to trade a little efficiency for lower ringing, and keep the loop on one layer with a close return.\"\n\nTip: Minimize the hot-loop area first; snubber and gate resistor are second-line.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's the trade-off of slowing the gate with a resistor?","Slower edges cut ringing and EMI but increase switching loss and heat, so you tune it to the minimum slowing that meets EMI."]}],
["Snubbers", "When and how do you use an RC snubber?", "**Why they ask:** practical ringing control.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"An RC snubber across a switching node or a diode damps the ringing by giving the resonant energy somewhere to dissipate. I size it by first measuring the ring frequency, estimating the parasitic capacitance by adding a known cap and seeing how the frequency shifts, then picking a resistor roughly equal to the characteristic impedance of that L-C and a capacitor a few times the parasitic. It costs some efficiency because the snubber dissipates power each cycle, so I use the smallest snubber that meets the EMI or voltage-stress target, and I treat it as a fix for ringing I couldn't fully remove by layout.\"\n\nTip: Measure the ring, find the parasitics by the cap-shift method, then set R to the L-C impedance.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why not just use a big snubber to kill all ringing?","It dissipates power every switching cycle, so an oversized snubber wastes efficiency and adds heat — use the minimum that meets the target."]}],
["Isolated topologies", "Compare flyback, forward, LLC and phase-shift full bridge.", "**Why they ask:** picking an isolated topology by power level.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Flyback is simplest and cheapest, stores energy in the transformer, and suits low power up to maybe 100 watts — think chargers. Forward transfers energy directly through the transformer, better for mid power. LLC resonant runs the switches with soft switching so it's very efficient and low-EMI, popular for a few hundred watts up into the kilowatts where efficiency matters, but it's harder to control over a wide range. Phase-shift full bridge is the workhorse for high power, with zero-voltage switching and good use of the transformer. I pick by power level, efficiency target, cost, and how wide the input and load range is.\"\n\nTip: Flyback (low power/cheap) → forward → LLC (efficient, mid-high) → phase-shift full bridge (high power).", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is LLC so efficient?","It uses resonance to switch at zero voltage, nearly eliminating switching loss, so efficiency stays high especially at high frequency."]}],
["Feedback isolation", "How do you close the feedback loop across an isolation barrier?", "**Why they ask:** isolated supplies need isolated feedback.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The error has to cross the barrier without breaking isolation. The classic way is an optocoupler with a TL431 shunt reference on the secondary: the TL431 senses the output, drives the opto, and the opto's transistor on the primary feeds the controller — simple and cheap, but the opto adds a pole and ages, so I account for its bandwidth and current-transfer-ratio spread in the loop. Alternatives are a primary-side auxiliary winding that senses output indirectly — no opto, lower cost, but poorer regulation — or a digital isolator. I choose by the regulation accuracy and cost needed.\"\n\nTip: Opto + TL431 is the standard; account for the opto's pole and CTR variation in the loop.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the downside of primary-side regulation?","It infers the output from the auxiliary winding rather than measuring it, so load and cable regulation are worse — fine for cheap chargers, not precision rails."]}],
["Compensation", "How do you compensate a converter's control loop?", "**Why they ask:** loop compensation is core power-electronics skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I shape the loop so it has enough gain at low frequency for regulation, a crossover well below the switching frequency — often a tenth or less — and adequate phase margin, usually 45 to 60 degrees, so it's stable and responds well to transients. I start from the power stage's poles and zeros: an output L-C gives a double pole and the output cap's ESR gives a zero. Then I add a compensator — a Type II for current-mode or a Type III when I need to overcome the L-C double-pole phase drop in voltage-mode — placing its poles and zeros to cancel the troublesome ones. I confirm with a measured Bode plot, not just the model.\"\n\nTip: Crossover below ~1/10 switching freq, 45–60° phase margin, Type II/III to shape poles and zeros.", "D", "X", null, {"t":1,"f":["When do you need a Type III compensator?","In voltage-mode control, where the output L-C double pole causes a fast 180° phase drop that a Type III's two zeros compensate; current-mode usually needs only Type II."]}],
["Loop measurement", "How do you measure a converter's loop stability on the bench?", "**Why they ask:** proving stability, not just simulating it.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I measure the loop gain with a frequency-response analyzer or a network analyzer set up for a Bode plot: I break the loop at a point where the impedance is high looking forward and low looking back — typically across a small series resistor in the feedback — inject a small signal there, and measure gain and phase versus frequency. From the plot I read the crossover frequency, the phase margin at crossover, and the gain margin. I check it across line, load and temperature because margins shrink at the corners. A good phase margin on paper means nothing until the bench Bode confirms it.\"\n\nTip: Inject across a feedback resistor, read phase margin at crossover, and verify across line/load/temperature.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What phase margin do you aim for?","Typically 45 to 60 degrees — enough to avoid ringing and instability while keeping good transient response; too much makes the loop sluggish."]}],
["Current sensing", "What are the ways to sense current in a converter, with trade-offs?", "**Why they ask:** current sensing enables control and protection.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A sense resistor is accurate and simple but burns power and adds loss at high current. Inductor DCR sensing uses the inductor's own resistance with an RC network — lossless but temperature-dependent and less accurate. A current-sense transformer works for AC/switched currents with no loss but can't sense DC and needs reset. Sensing the MOSFET's on-resistance drop is lossless and cheap but varies with temperature and part. For high current I lean toward DCR or a transformer to avoid the resistor loss; for accuracy-critical protection I use a real shunt, often with a dedicated sense amplifier.\"\n\nTip: Shunt = accurate but lossy; DCR = lossless but drifts with temperature; CT = lossless AC-only.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does DCR sensing drift?","Copper resistance rises with temperature by about 0.4% per degree, so the sensed current scales with the inductor's temperature unless compensated."]}],
["Control modes", "Current-mode versus voltage-mode control — how do you choose?", "**Why they ask:** a fundamental control decision.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Voltage-mode controls duty cycle from the output voltage error alone — simple, good noise immunity, but the L-C double pole makes compensation harder and it responds slower to input changes. Current-mode adds an inner loop sensing inductor current, which simplifies the outer loop to effectively first-order, gives cycle-by-cycle current limiting for free, handles input transients faster via feed-forward, and eases paralleling. Its downsides are noise sensitivity on the current sense and a need for slope compensation above 50% duty. I usually prefer current-mode for its built-in current limit and easier loop, choosing voltage-mode when the current signal is too noisy.\"\n\nTip: Current-mode gives easier loop, inherent current limit and fast line response; needs slope comp above 50% duty.", "D", "X", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why does current-mode need slope compensation?","Above 50% duty, peak current-mode control develops a sub-harmonic oscillation; adding an artificial ramp (slope compensation) stabilizes it."]}],
["PFC", "What is power factor correction and when is it required?", "**Why they ask:** AC-input supplies above a power threshold need PFC.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A plain rectifier-and-capacitor front end draws current in narrow spikes at the AC peaks, which has poor power factor and high harmonic distortion that stresses the grid. Power factor correction — usually a boost converter whose controller forces the input current to follow the sinusoidal voltage — makes the load look resistive, bringing power factor near one and meeting harmonic limits like IEC 61000-3-2. It's required above roughly 75 watts for many product classes. I design the PFC boost to hold a regulated high-voltage bus, then a downstream DC-DC makes the final rails, and I size it for hold-up and the harmonic standard.\"\n\nTip: PFC (a boost shaping input current to the voltage) makes the input look resistive and meets harmonic limits — needed above ~75W.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is a boost the usual PFC topology?","Its input inductor is in series with the line, so the controller can shape a continuous input current to track the rectified sine, which is hard in a buck."]}],
["Output caps", "How do you select output capacitors for a switching regulator?", "**Why they ask:** output cap choice sets ripple and transient response.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I size them for three things: steady-state ripple, transient response, and stability. Ripple voltage comes from the capacitance, the ESR, and the inductor ripple current — at high frequency the ESR times the ripple current often dominates, so a low-ESR part matters. For a load step, the cap supplies current until the loop responds, so I need enough capacitance and low ESR to hold the output within the allowed droop. ESR also places a zero that affects the loop. I often mix a bulk cap for transient and smaller ceramics for high-frequency ripple, watching ceramic DC-bias derating.\"\n\nTip: ESR often dominates ripple; capacitance and ESR together set transient droop — and ceramics lose capacitance under DC bias.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why worry about ceramic DC-bias derating?","A class-II ceramic can lose a large fraction of its rated capacitance at its rated voltage, so the effective capacitance in-circuit is far below the datasheet number."]}],
["Right-half-plane zero", "What is a right-half-plane zero and which topologies have it?", "**Why they ask:** the RHP zero limits boost and flyback bandwidth.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Boost and flyback converters in continuous conduction have a right-half-plane zero in their control-to-output response. It's a zero that adds gain but subtracts phase, like a pole — the physical cause is that to deliver more output you first widen the switch on-time, which momentarily takes energy away from the output before it builds up, so the output initially dips when you ask for more. You can't compensate it away, so you have to set the loop crossover well below the RHP zero frequency, which limits how fast these converters can respond. It moves with load and input, so I design for the worst case.\"\n\nTip: RHP zero adds gain but loses phase; you can't cancel it, so you crossover well below it — limiting boost/flyback bandwidth.", "D", "X", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why does a buck not have an RHP zero?","In a buck, increasing duty immediately sends more energy to the output, so there's no initial reverse response — the zero is in the left half-plane or absent."]}],
["Layout", "What are the layout priorities for a switching power supply?", "**Why they ask:** power layout makes or breaks a design.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First, minimize the high-di/dt hot loops — the input cap to the switches in a buck, the switch-diode-output loop in a boost — because their inductance causes ringing, EMI and voltage spikes. Second, Kelvin-sense critical nodes, especially the current sense and the feedback, so switching currents don't corrupt the measurement. Third, keep the feedback trace short and away from the switch node. Fourth, give the power components a good thermal path and plenty of copper. Fifth, place decoupling and gate-drive loops tight. Good power layout is mostly about tiny hot loops and clean sensing.\"\n\nTip: Tiny hot loops, Kelvin sensing, short quiet feedback — in that priority.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What is Kelvin sensing and why use it on a shunt?","Taking the sense connection right at the resistor's pads, separate from the current path, so IR drop in the power traces doesn't add to the measured voltage."]}],
["Hold-up", "What is hold-up time and how do you design for it?", "**Why they ask:** AC supplies must ride through brief dropouts.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Hold-up time is how long the supply keeps its outputs in regulation after the AC input disappears — a requirement like 20 milliseconds so the system survives a missed mains cycle. The energy comes from the bulk capacitor on the high-voltage bus; I size it from the energy equation, since the usable energy is half C times the difference of the squares of the starting bus voltage and the minimum voltage the downstream converter still works at. A wider allowed droop lets me use a smaller, cheaper cap. So I design the downstream stage to keep regulating down to a low bus voltage, which shrinks the bulk cap needed for the hold-up spec.\"\n\nTip: Hold-up energy is in the bulk cap; a lower usable bus voltage shrinks the cap for the same hold-up time.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How does allowing a lower bus voltage help?","Usable energy scales with the difference of the squares of start and minimum voltage, so a lower minimum extracts far more energy from the same cap."]}],
["Light load", "How do you keep efficiency high at light load?", "**Why they ask:** standby and light-load efficiency are regulated and matter.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"At light load the fixed losses — switching loss, gate charge, controller quiescent current, and the energy wasted driving the synchronous FET — dominate, so continuous high-frequency switching is wasteful. The main tool is a pulse-skipping or pulse-frequency-modulation mode where the converter switches only as often as needed, cutting switching loss dramatically at low current, plus diode emulation so the sync FET doesn't let current flow backward. I also pick a controller with low quiescent current and may reduce switching frequency at light load. The trade-off is slightly higher output ripple and a variable frequency, which I check against EMI and noise needs.\"\n\nTip: Pulse-skipping/PFM plus diode emulation cut the fixed switching losses that dominate at light load.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does the synchronous FET hurt light-load efficiency?","Without diode emulation it conducts current backward during the off cycle, circulating energy and wasting it; diode emulation turns it off when current reaches zero."]}],
["Thermal resistance", "How do you do a thermal calculation for a power MOSFET?", "**Why they ask:** thermal design keeps parts alive.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I estimate the device's total power loss — conduction loss, which is current squared times on-resistance at the hot on-resistance, plus switching loss from the overlap of voltage and current during transitions times frequency. Then I use the thermal resistance path: junction to case, case to heatsink through the interface material, and heatsink to ambient, adding them to get junction-to-ambient. Junction temperature is ambient plus power times that total thermal resistance. I keep the junction below its rating with margin, derate for the real maximum ambient, and if it's too hot I add a heatsink, more copper, or a lower-loss part.\"\n\nTip: Tj = Tambient + Ploss × Rθ(j-a); use hot Rds(on) and include switching loss.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why use the hot Rds(on), not the datasheet 25°C value?","On-resistance rises substantially with temperature, so conduction loss and junction temperature feed back on each other — using the cold value under-predicts the heat."]}],
["Protection", "Explain OVP, OCP, UVLO and OTP and how they interact.", "**Why they ask:** layered protection is expected in any supply.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Under-voltage lockout keeps the controller off until its supply is high enough to drive the switches properly, preventing a weak, damaging start. Over-current protection limits or shuts down on excess current — cycle-by-cycle limiting, hiccup, or latch — to survive a short. Over-voltage protection catches a feedback failure or a load dump before the output damages downstream parts. Over-temperature protection shuts down or folds back if the die gets too hot. They have to coordinate: the start-up sequence respects UVLO, OCP allows inrush without false trips, and recovery behavior — auto-restart versus latch — matches the application's safety needs.\"\n\nTip: UVLO for clean start, OCP for shorts, OVP for feedback faults, OTP for heat — coordinated so normal operation doesn't false-trip.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's hiccup mode in over-current protection?","The converter shuts down briefly on over-current then retries periodically, limiting average power into a fault so it stays cool until the short clears."]}]
]);

// Power Electronics Engineer. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["hw-power"] = (QB["hw-power"] || []).concat([
["Creepage and clearance", "What are creepage and clearance, and how do they affect an isolated supply?", "**Why they ask:** safety isolation is non-negotiable.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Clearance is the shortest distance through air between two conductors; creepage is the shortest path along a surface. Safety standards set minimum creepage and clearance based on the working voltage, the insulation level — functional, basic or reinforced — the pollution degree, and the material. For a mains supply with reinforced isolation between primary and secondary, that can be several millimeters, which drives the transformer construction, the opto's rating, and board slots or cutouts under the barrier. I design the layout to meet these from the start, because a violation fails safety certification outright.\"\n\nTip: Clearance is through air, creepage along the surface; working voltage and insulation class set the minimums.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why add a slot in the PCB under the isolation barrier?","It increases the creepage distance along the surface without widening the whole board, helping meet reinforced-isolation requirements."]}],
["EMI filter", "How do you design the input EMI filter for a switching supply?", "**Why they ask:** nearly every supply needs one to pass emissions.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat differential-mode and common-mode noise separately because they need different elements. Differential-mode noise, between the lines, is filtered with X-capacitors and the leakage inductance of the choke. Common-mode noise, line-to-earth, is filtered with a common-mode choke whose windings cancel for the main current but present high impedance to common-mode, plus Y-capacitors to earth — which are limited by leakage-current safety limits. I measure the noise with a LISN to see which mode dominates, size the filter to the gap between my emissions and the limit, and keep the filter layout tight so it doesn't couple noise around itself.\"\n\nTip: X-caps and leakage L for differential mode; common-mode choke and Y-caps for common mode — Y-caps limited by leakage current.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why are Y-capacitors limited in value?","They connect line to earth, so their current flows in the safety ground; too large a Y-cap exceeds the leakage-current safety limit and can trip RCDs."]}],
["Transient response", "How do you test and improve a supply's load-step response?", "**Why they ask:** transient behavior is a top real-world requirement.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I apply a fast load step with an electronic load — say from 10% to 90% with a controlled slew — and capture the output on a scope to see the voltage droop, the recovery time, and any ringing. The initial droop is set by the output capacitance and ESR before the loop reacts; the recovery and overshoot are set by the loop bandwidth and phase margin. To improve it I raise the crossover frequency if phase margin allows, add output capacitance or lower ESR to cut the initial droop, or add feed-forward. I measure at the device's pins, with a tight ground, because probing technique easily fakes extra ringing.\"\n\nTip: Initial droop = caps/ESR; recovery = loop bandwidth — and probe at the pins to avoid measurement artifacts.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does probe technique matter when measuring ripple and transients?","A long ground lead picks up switching noise and makes the output look far noisier than it is; use a tip-and-barrel or short spring ground."]}],
["Ripple measurement", "How do you correctly measure output ripple and noise?", "**Why they ask:** it's easy to measure this wrong.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I measure right at the output pins with a very short ground connection — the spring-tip or tip-and-barrel on the probe, not the long ground clip — because the clip's loop picks up switch-node radiation and exaggerates the noise. I limit the scope bandwidth appropriately to the spec, usually 20 MHz for low-frequency ripple, and separately look at the high-frequency switching spikes with full bandwidth. I note the measurement conditions — load, input, bandwidth — because ripple numbers are meaningless without them. The switching ripple at the fundamental and the narrow high-frequency spikes are different things I report separately.\"\n\nTip: Short ground, measure at the pins, state the bandwidth — most 'noise' is bad probing.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why limit scope bandwidth to 20 MHz for ripple?","To measure the fundamental ripple as a datasheet typically specs it, without masking it under broadband probe-pickup noise — the HF spikes are characterized separately."]}],
["Magnetics saturation", "What is core saturation and how do you avoid it?", "**Why they ask:** a saturated inductor can destroy a converter.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A magnetic core saturates when the flux density exceeds the material's limit — beyond that the inductance collapses and the current rises almost unchecked, which can blow the switch. Flux density rises with the amp-turns, so saturation happens at high current, low frequency, or during a fault or hard transient. I design so the peak current, including the ripple and any transient or startup inrush, keeps flux below the saturation limit with margin, derating as the core heats since saturation flux drops with temperature. I verify by looking at the inductor current on a scope — a sudden upward curl at the peak is the signature of impending saturation.\"\n\nTip: Saturation collapses inductance and runs current away; keep peak flux (incl. transients) below Bsat with thermal margin.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What does saturation look like on the inductor current waveform?","The otherwise-straight current ramp curls sharply upward near its peak, because falling inductance lets current rise faster."]}],
["Core loss", "What drives core loss in a transformer or inductor?", "**Why they ask:** magnetic losses affect efficiency and heat.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Core loss depends on the flux swing, the switching frequency, and the material, and it rises steeply with both flux and frequency — roughly captured by the Steinmetz relationship where loss grows with flux and frequency to some power greater than one. So pushing frequency up to shrink the magnetics eventually costs more in core loss. I pick a core material suited to the frequency, keep the flux swing moderate, and balance core loss against copper loss, since more turns cut flux but add winding resistance. At high frequency I also watch proximity and skin effect in the windings, which is a separate copper-loss mechanism.\"\n\nTip: Core loss climbs faster than linearly with flux and frequency — balance it against copper loss and pick the right material.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the trade between turns and loss?","More turns lower the flux and core loss but raise winding resistance and copper loss; you optimize the two together for the operating point."]}],
["Transformer design", "How do you set the turns ratio and primary inductance of a flyback transformer?", "**Why they ask:** flyback magnetics design is a core competency.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The turns ratio comes from the input-to-output voltage relationship and the duty cycle I want at minimum input, keeping the reflected voltage and the switch's voltage stress within the MOSFET rating. The primary inductance sets the ripple current and whether it runs in continuous or discontinuous mode: a larger inductance gives lower peak current and CCM at full load, a smaller one gives DCM. I size it so peak current stays within the switch and core limits across the line and load range, pick a core that handles the energy without saturating, and set the air gap to store the energy. Then I verify stress, loss and temperature on the bench.\"\n\nTip: Turns ratio from voltage/duty and switch stress; primary inductance from peak current and CCM/DCM choice.", "D", "X", null, {"f":["Why does a flyback transformer need an air gap?","It stores energy in the gap each cycle; without a gap the core saturates quickly because a flyback uses the transformer as a coupled inductor, not just a transformer."]}],
["Wide-bandgap", "What's different about driving GaN and SiC devices versus silicon?", "**Why they ask:** wide-bandgap is increasingly common.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"They switch much faster and have lower losses, which is the attraction, but the fast edges make gate drive and layout far less forgiving. GaN has a low, narrow gate-voltage window, so I need a driver with tight voltage control and almost no overshoot, and minimal gate-loop inductance or it rings and over-volts the gate. The ultra-fast switching node radiates and rings more, so the hot-loop layout has to be even tighter. SiC tolerates higher temperature and voltage but often wants a negative off-gate bias to prevent false turn-on from fast dv/dt. In short: the devices are great, but the gate drive and layout discipline must be excellent.\"\n\nTip: Wide-bandgap pays off only with tight gate-loop layout, precise gate drive, and attention to dv/dt-induced turn-on.", "D", "X", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why might a SiC FET need negative gate bias when off?","Its fast dv/dt can couple through the gate-drain capacitance and momentarily turn it on; a negative off-bias keeps the gate safely below threshold."]}],
["Paralleling", "What do you watch for when paralleling MOSFETs or converters?", "**Why they ask:** high-current designs parallel devices and phases.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"For paralleled MOSFETs the concern is current sharing. On-resistance has a positive temperature coefficient, so a FET carrying more current heats up, its resistance rises, and it sheds current back — that's self-balancing for conduction, which helps. Switching sharing is trickier and depends on matched gate drive and symmetric layout, so I keep gate-loop and power-loop lengths equal and sometimes add small gate resistors. For paralleling whole converters or phases, I use current-mode control or an explicit current-share bus and droop so no phase hogs the load. Symmetry in layout is the recurring theme.\"\n\nTip: Rds(on)'s positive tempco self-balances conduction; switching sharing needs symmetric layout and matched gate drive.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How do multiphase VRMs share current between phases?","A controller senses each phase's current and adjusts duty, often with a droop/load-line, so the phases carry equal current and interleave to cut ripple."]}],
["Battery charging", "How does a CC-CV battery charger work?", "**Why they ask:** charging shows up in many products.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Constant-current, constant-voltage charging has two phases. In constant-current the charger pushes a fixed current into the battery and the voltage climbs as it fills — this does the bulk of the charging. When the voltage reaches the battery's target, like 4.2 volts per lithium cell, it switches to constant-voltage: it holds that voltage and the current naturally tapers as the cell approaches full. Charging stops when the current falls below a termination threshold. I respect the cell's limits — current, voltage and temperature — because over-voltage or charging a cold or hot lithium cell is a safety hazard, so a real charger adds temperature qualification and protection.\"\n\nTip: CC fills most of the charge, CV tops off with tapering current — and lithium needs strict voltage/temperature limits for safety.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why is it dangerous to charge a lithium cell below freezing?","Charging a cold lithium cell causes lithium plating on the anode, which permanently reduces capacity and can create internal shorts — a safety risk."]}],
["LDO vs switcher", "When would you use a linear regulator instead of a switcher?", "**Why they ask:** picking the right regulator per rail.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"An LDO dissipates the voltage difference times the current as heat, so it's only efficient when the drop is small or the current is low, but it's quiet, cheap, small, and fast — no switching noise. I use an LDO for a low-noise rail feeding analog, RF or a PLL where switcher ripple would hurt, for a small drop from an already-regulated rail, or where board space and simplicity win. I use a switcher when the voltage drop and current make linear losses and heat unacceptable. A common pattern is a switcher to get close, then a small LDO to clean up a sensitive rail.\"\n\nTip: LDO for low-noise, small-drop, low-current rails; switcher when the drop×current heat is too high. Often switcher-then-LDO.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What is PSRR and why does it matter for an LDO?","Power-supply rejection ratio measures how well the LDO blocks input ripple from reaching its output — high PSRR is why an LDO cleans up a switcher's noise for analog loads."]}],
["Derating", "How do you apply component derating in a power design?", "**Why they ask:** derating drives reliability.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Derating means operating components below their absolute ratings so they last and tolerate worst case. I keep capacitor voltage, especially electrolytics and ceramics, well under rated — often 20% or more margin — because voltage and ripple current drive their wear and lifetime. Semiconductors run below their voltage and current ratings with thermal margin on junction temperature. Resistors run below rated power. I apply the company's or industry derating guidelines across the worst-case line, load and temperature, not nominal. Derating trades a bit of cost and size for a large gain in field reliability and margin against part spread.\"\n\nTip: Keep voltage, current and temperature well below ratings at worst case — especially electrolytic caps, which wear with ripple and heat.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why do electrolytic capacitors need the most derating attention?","Their lifetime roughly halves for every 10°C rise and they wear from ripple-current heating, so voltage and ripple derating plus temperature directly set how long they last."]}],
["Reliability", "How do you estimate and improve a power supply's reliability?", "**Why they ask:** reliability is often the product's weak point.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I identify the lifetime-limiting parts — usually electrolytic capacitors and the hottest semiconductors — and estimate their life from the actual operating temperature and ripple, since cap life roughly halves every 10 degrees. A reliability prediction or an FMEA highlights the risky items, and I improve the weakest links: run the electrolytics cooler, derate them, or switch to longer-life or film/ceramic parts; improve cooling on hot devices; and add protection against the fault modes that cause early failure. Then HALT and thermal testing confirm the real margins rather than trusting the model alone.\"\n\nTip: Find the lifetime-limiting part — usually a hot electrolytic cap — and cool, derate or replace it; confirm with HALT.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the typical reliability weak link in a supply?","The electrolytic capacitors — their wear-out from heat and ripple usually sets the supply's end-of-life before semiconductors fail."]}],
["Line and load regulation", "Define line and load regulation and what degrades them.", "**Why they ask:** basic spec every power engineer must know.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Line regulation is how much the output changes as the input voltage varies over its range; load regulation is how much it changes as the load varies from light to full. Both are driven mainly by the feedback loop's DC gain — more low-frequency loop gain means tighter regulation — and by where you sense the output. Load regulation suffers from resistance between the regulator and the load, which is why remote or Kelvin sensing at the load point helps. For isolated supplies, primary-side regulation inherently has worse load regulation because it infers the output. I quote both across the full operating range.\"\n\nTip: High DC loop gain tightens both; sense at the load (remote sense) to fix load regulation across trace drop.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How does remote sensing improve load regulation?","It measures voltage at the load instead of the regulator, so the loop compensates for the IR drop in the distribution path."]}],
["Standby power", "How do you minimize no-load standby power in an AC adapter?", "**Why they ask:** standby power is regulated (e.g. efficiency standards).\n\n**Answer:**\n\"At no load almost all the input power is loss, and regulations cap standby to small fractions of a watt, so I attack every fixed drain. I use a controller with burst or deep-sleep mode that wakes only occasionally at no load, minimize the controller's startup and housekeeping current, reduce the bleeder and feedback-divider currents, limit X-cap discharge resistor loss, and cut transformer and snubber losses. Primary-side regulation helps because it drops the opto's standby current. I measure at the wall with a precision power meter to confirm I meet the standby limit.\"\n\nTip: Burst-mode control plus trimming every fixed bias and bleeder current gets standby into the milliwatts.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does burst mode help at no load?","It lets the converter sleep most of the time and switch only in short bursts to top up the output, slashing the switching and quiescent losses that dominate no-load power."]}],
["Worst-case analysis", "How do you do worst-case analysis on a power design?", "**Why they ask:** robust designs survive the corners, not just nominal.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I check that the design still meets spec with every relevant parameter at its worst extreme simultaneously — minimum and maximum input voltage, full and no load, temperature extremes, and component tolerances including initial accuracy, temperature drift, and aging. For each requirement I find which direction of each variable is worst and stack them. For example, maximum junction temperature happens at high load, high ambient, and the high end of on-resistance together. Loop stability I check at the corner that gives the least phase margin. It catches the designs that work on the bench at room temperature but fail a fraction of units in the field.\"\n\nTip: Stack every parameter at its worst-case extreme simultaneously, per requirement — nominal passing isn't enough.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why isn't a passing bench prototype enough?","One bench unit at room temperature doesn't cover tolerance spread, temperature, and line/load corners where a fraction of production units will land."]}],
["PMBus", "What is PMBus and why would a supply have digital telemetry?", "**Why they ask:** digital power is common in servers and systems.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"PMBus is a standard digital interface, built on I2C, for configuring and monitoring power converters. It lets the system read voltage, current, power and temperature from each rail, set the output voltage and margins, read and clear faults, and sequence rails in software. In a server or complex board that's valuable: you can monitor health, log faults, tune rails without changing hardware, and do controlled power sequencing across many supplies. The trade-off is added firmware and a controller that supports it, so I use it where the monitoring, sequencing and field-telemetry benefits justify the complexity.\"\n\nTip: PMBus brings telemetry, software-set voltages, fault logging and sequencing — valuable in multi-rail systems.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's a practical benefit of power telemetry in the field?","You can detect a rail drifting or a fan/thermal problem before it fails, and diagnose issues remotely from logged voltage, current and temperature."]}],
["Scenario", "A supply passes at room temperature but a few units shut down when hot in the field. How do you investigate?", "**Why they ask:** real debugging of a marginal design.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Hot-only, some-units behavior points to thermal protection tripping or a parameter drifting with temperature on the weaker units. I'd reproduce it in a thermal chamber across the full temperature and the line/load corners, and instrument temperatures and the key nodes. I'd check whether over-temperature protection is firing — if so, which component is hottest and why — or whether the loop loses phase margin hot, or a current limit tightens, or an electrolytic's ESR rises and causes instability. Then I compare failing versus passing units for the marginal parameter. The fix is recovering thermal or stability margin at worst case, not just making the bench unit pass.\"\n\nTip: Reproduce across temperature and corners, find whether it's OTP, loop margin, or a drifting part — then recover worst-case margin.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why do only some units fail when hot?","Component tolerance spread — those units sit at the unfavorable end of a parameter, so the same thermal stress pushes them past a limit the others still clear."]}]
]);

