QB["hw-power"] = (QB["hw-power"] || []).concat([
["Soft start", "Why do converters need soft start, and how is it implemented?", "**Why they ask:** startup behavior affects stress and inrush.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Without soft start, a converter tries to bring the output up instantly, which charges the output capacitance through a huge inrush current and can trip over-current, sag the input, or stress the switches. Soft start ramps the reference or the duty cycle up gradually over milliseconds so the output rises in a controlled slope and the inrush is bounded. It's usually done with an internal ramp on the reference or an external soft-start capacitor that sets the ramp time. I set the ramp slow enough to limit inrush and avoid tripping protection, but fast enough to meet the system's power-up timing and sequencing needs.\"\n\nTip: Soft start ramps the reference/duty so the output rises gradually, bounding inrush and avoiding false over-current trips.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How does soft start interact with a downstream load that has its own inrush?","You coordinate the ramp and any sequencing so the upstream supply's current limit isn't tripped by the load's own startup surge."]}],
["Duty cycle limits", "Why do minimum and maximum duty-cycle limits matter?", "**Why they ask:** real controllers have timing limits that constrain the design.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A switcher can't make an arbitrarily short or long on-time. Minimum on-time limits how high the input-to-output ratio can go — a buck stepping 48 volts to 1 volt at a high frequency may need an on-time shorter than the controller can produce, forcing pulse-skipping or a lower frequency. Maximum duty, and the need to refresh a bootstrap, limits how close the output can approach the input in a buck. I check that my required duty at both input extremes stays within the controller's min and max on-time and off-time, and if not I change frequency, topology, or pick a controller rated for the conversion ratio.\"\n\nTip: Min on-time caps the step-down ratio at high frequency; max duty caps how near the output gets to the input — check both at the input extremes.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What do you do if the needed on-time is below the controller's minimum?","Lower the switching frequency, pick a controller with a shorter min on-time, or use a topology better suited to the large conversion ratio."]}],
["Efficiency measurement", "How do you accurately measure converter efficiency?", "**Why they ask:** efficiency claims must be measured correctly.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Efficiency is output power divided by input power, so I measure input voltage and current and output voltage and current simultaneously with accurate meters, sensing voltage right at the converter's terminals so I don't count wiring drop as loss — four-wire Kelvin connections matter here because a small voltage error at high current is a big power error. I sweep load and input, and I let the unit reach thermal steady state because losses change with temperature. I watch meter accuracy and timing alignment of the four readings. Then I plot efficiency versus load to show the full-load and light-load behavior, not just one point.\"\n\nTip: Kelvin-sense voltages at the terminals, measure all four quantities accurately at thermal steady state, and sweep load.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why Kelvin-sense the voltages for efficiency?","At high current, even a few millivolts of lead drop is significant power; sensing at the terminals keeps wiring loss out of the efficiency number."]}]
]);
